<!-- ============ RENTING RULES BY COUNTRY ============ -->
<section class="rules-wrap" id="rulesSection">
  <div class="rules-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Know before you sign</span>
    <h2>Renting rules where you live</h2>
    <p>Deposits, notice periods and tenant protections vary a lot by country. Pick yours, or let us guess from your location.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="rules-controls">
    <select class="rules-select" id="rulesCountrySelect" aria-label="Choose your country">
      <option value="uk">United Kingdom</option>
      <option value="usa">United States</option>
      <option value="france">France</option>
      <option value="germany">Germany</option>
      <option value="uae">United Arab Emirates</option>
      <option value="canada">Canada</option>
      <option value="other">Somewhere else</option>
    </select>
    <button type="button" class="rules-detect" id="rulesDetectBtn">
      <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/><path d="M12 2v3M12 19v3M2 12h3M19 12h3"/></svg>
      Use my location
    </button>
    <p class="rules-status" id="rulesStatus" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  </div>

  <!-- UNITED KINGDOM -->
  <div class="rules-panel" data-country="uk">
    <div class="rules-panel-head"><h3>United Kingdom</h3><span class="rules-checked">Checked Sept 2026 · England</span></div>
    <div class="rules-grid">
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Deposit</h5><p>Capped at 5 weeks' rent (6 weeks if annual rent is over £50,000). Must be placed in a government-approved protection scheme <span class="muted">(TDS, mydeposits or DPS)</span> within 30 days.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Upfront fees</h5><p>The Tenant Fees Act 2019 bans most letting fees. A landlord or agent can only ask for rent, the deposit, and a holding deposit capped at 1 week's rent.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Ending a tenancy</h5><p>Since the Renters' Rights Act took effect on 1 May 2026, "no-fault" Section 21 eviction no longer exists in England. Tenancies are periodic, and a landlord must use a Section 8 ground to seek possession.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Rent increases</h5><p>Under the same reforms, rent on an existing tenancy can only be increased once every 12 months, with proper written notice.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="rules-note">Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland run their own separate tenancy systems — this summary covers England only. This is general guidance, not legal advice; check <span class="muted">gov.uk</span> or a free adviser like Shelter or Citizens Advice for your situation.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- UNITED STATES -->
  <div class="rules-panel" data-country="usa">
    <div class="rules-panel-head"><h3>United States</h3><span class="rules-checked">Checked Sept 2026</span></div>
    <div class="rules-grid">
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>No single national law</h5><p>Landlord–tenant law is set state by state, and often city by city on top of that. Deposit caps, notice periods and rent control (where it exists at all) vary widely.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Federal baseline</h5><p>The Fair Housing Act bans discrimination in renting, and most states recognise an implied "warranty of habitability" — a landlord's duty to keep the place livable.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Typical pattern</h5><p>Where deposits are capped at all, 1–2 months' rent is common. A month-to-month tenancy usually needs 30 days' notice to end, though this varies by state.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Eviction</h5><p>A landlord can't legally change the locks or remove belongings without a court order — that's true in every state, regardless of what the lease says.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="rules-note">Because the details depend entirely on your state (and sometimes city), check your state attorney general's tenant guide, HUD.gov, or a local tenants' rights organization before relying on any of this.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- FRANCE -->
  <div class="rules-panel" data-country="france">
    <div class="rules-panel-head"><h3>France</h3><span class="rules-checked">Checked Sept 2026</span></div>
    <div class="rules-grid">
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Dépôt de garantie</h5><p>Capped at 1 month's rent for an unfurnished let, or 2 months' for furnished. The landlord has up to 1 month to return it (2 months if deductions apply) after the "état des lieux de sortie".</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Agency fees</h5><p>Under the loi ALUR, certain agency fees are shared between tenant and landlord, and the tenant's share is capped per square metre depending on the area.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Giving notice</h5><p>A tenant can generally leave with 1 month's notice in a designated tense housing zone (3 months elsewhere for unfurnished lets), or 1 month for a furnished let.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Landlord ending the lease</h5><p>A landlord can only decline to renew on specific legal grounds, and typically owes 6 months' notice before the end of an unfurnished lease.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="rules-note">Governed mainly by the loi du 6 juillet 1989. This is general guidance, not legal advice — an ADIL (Agence Départementale d'Information sur le Logement) offers free local advice, or check service-public.fr.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- GERMANY -->
  <div class="rules-panel" data-country="germany">
    <div class="rules-panel-head"><h3>Germany</h3><span class="rules-checked">Checked Sept 2026</span></div>
    <div class="rules-grid">
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Kaution (deposit)</h5><p>Capped at 3 months' cold rent (Kaltmiete, excluding utilities), payable in up to 3 monthly instalments, and must be held separately from the landlord's own money.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Mietpreisbremse</h5><p>In designated tight housing markets, rent on a new contract can't be set more than 10% above the local comparable rent (Mietspiegel). This rent brake has been extended to run until 31 December 2029.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Giving notice</h5><p>A tenant can typically end an open-ended tenancy with 3 months' written notice.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Landlord ending the lease</h5><p>A landlord needs a recognised reason — personal use (Eigenbedarf) is the most common — and owes longer notice the longer you've lived there.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="rules-note">Set out in the Bürgerliches Gesetzbuch (BGB), §535–577a — among the strongest tenant protections in Europe. Joining a local Mieterverein (tenants' association), such as the Deutscher Mieterbund, is common practice and gives access to affordable legal advice before you sign anything.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- UAE -->
  <div class="rules-panel" data-country="uae">
    <div class="rules-panel-head"><h3>United Arab Emirates</h3><span class="rules-checked">Checked Sept 2026 · Dubai</span></div>
    <div class="rules-grid">
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Ejari registration</h5><p>Every residential tenancy contract in Dubai must be registered through Ejari. An unregistered contract can cause problems with utilities, visas and disputes later.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Deposit</h5><p>Market practice, rather than a fixed legal cap, is typically around 5% of the annual rent for an unfurnished property, or 10% for furnished.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Renewal increases</h5><p>Governed by the RERA Rental Index: the allowed increase ranges from 0% to 20%, based on how far your current rent sits below the average for comparable properties nearby.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Notice of increase</h5><p>A landlord must give 90 days' written notice before a rent increase takes effect at renewal.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="rules-note">This summary covers Dubai; Abu Dhabi and the other emirates run their own separate rental systems. Check the official rent calculator on the Dubai Land Department site (dubailand.gov.ae) or the Dubai REST app.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- CANADA -->
  <div class="rules-panel" data-country="canada">
    <div class="rules-panel-head"><h3>Canada</h3><span class="rules-checked">Checked Sept 2026</span></div>
    <div class="rules-grid">
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>No single national law</h5><p>Residential tenancies are regulated separately by each province and territory — deposit rules, rent control and the eviction process all differ.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Deposits</h5><p>Most provinces ban or tightly restrict deposits beyond a "last month's rent" payment held toward the final month.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Ending a tenancy</h5><p>A landlord generally can't remove a tenant without an order from the provincial tribunal — for example Ontario's Landlord and Tenant Board or BC's Residential Tenancy Branch.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Rent increases</h5><p>Some provinces (e.g. Ontario, BC) cap annual increases on an existing tenancy with a published guideline percentage; others (e.g. Alberta) have no such cap.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="rules-note">Search "[your province] residential tenancies act" or your provincial Landlord and Tenant Board / Residential Tenancy Branch for the rules that actually apply to you.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- OTHER / FALLBACK -->
  <div class="rules-panel" data-country="other">
    <div class="rules-panel-head"><h3>Somewhere else</h3><span class="rules-checked">General guidance</span></div>
    <div class="rules-grid">
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Start here</h5><p>Check whether your country requires deposits to be capped or protected, sets a minimum notice period, and requires a court or tribunal order before eviction.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Find a local body</h5><p>Most countries have either a national tenants' union or a consumer-protection agency that publishes a plain-language renting guide.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Before you sign</h5><p>Get the deposit amount, notice period and any renewal terms in writing, and keep a dated record of the property's condition when you move in.</p></div>
      <div class="rules-item"><h5>Close to one of ours?</h5><p>If your country is close to one of the ones in the dropdown above, that page is a reasonable starting point — but always verify locally.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="rules-note">This is general guidance only, not legal advice, and isn't specific to any one country. Please verify with a local tenants' advice service before relying on it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

